---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["how-to-calculate-pto-accrual"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    PTO accrual is paid time off you earn gradually as you work, rather than
    all at once. The usual formula is
    <strong>annual PTO hours &divide; annual hours worked</strong>, which gives
    an accrual rate per hour worked. Two weeks (80 hours) a year on a
    2,080-hour full-time schedule accrues at 80 &divide; 2,080 =
    <strong>0.0385 hours</strong> for every hour you work, or about 3.08 hours
    per two-week pay period.
  </p>

  <h2>The two common methods</h2>
  <h3>1. Per hour worked</h3>
  <p>
    <code>accrual rate = annual PTO hours &divide; annual scheduled hours</code><br />
    <code>PTO earned = hours worked &times; accrual rate</code>
  </p>
  <p>
    This method suits hourly and part-time staff, because PTO follows the
    hours you actually work. A week of overtime earns a bit more (if your
    policy counts overtime hours), and a short week earns less.
  </p>
  <h3>2. Per pay period</h3>
  <p>
    <code>PTO per pay period = annual PTO hours &divide; pay periods per year</code>
  </p>
  <p>
    Common for salaried staff: the same amount is added every paycheck
    regardless of hours. For 80 hours a year, that's 1.54 hours a week, 3.08
    biweekly, 3.33 semimonthly or 6.67 monthly.
  </p>

  <h2>Accrual rates at a glance</h2>
  <p>For a full-time schedule of 40 hours a week (2,080 hours a year):</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>PTO per year</th><th>Per hour worked</th><th>Per biweekly period (80 h)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>40 hours (1 week)</td><td>0.0192</td><td>1.54 hours</td></tr>
      <tr><td>80 hours (2 weeks)</td><td>0.0385</td><td>3.08 hours</td></tr>
      <tr><td>120 hours (3 weeks)</td><td>0.0577</td><td>4.62 hours</td></tr>
      <tr><td>160 hours (4 weeks)</td><td>0.0769</td><td>6.15 hours</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Keep at least four decimal places in the rate. Rounding 0.03846 to 0.04
    looks harmless, but over 2,080 hours it gives 83.2 hours instead of 80.
  </p>

  <h2>Worked examples</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Part-time.</strong> An employee on the 80-hour plan works 25 hours
    a week. Using the per-hour method: 25 &times; 52 = 1,300 hours a year
    &times; 0.0385 = <strong>50 hours</strong> of PTO a year, the same
    share of their schedule as a full-timer's 80 hours.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What it's worth.</strong> At $22.00 an hour, a full year's 80
    hours of PTO is worth 80 &times; $22.00 = <strong>$1,760</strong>. That's
    also the gap between your hourly rate and your effective rate if you
    compare a job with PTO against one without; the
    <a href="/calculators/salary-to-hourly-calculator/">Salary to Hourly
    Calculator</a> can adjust for it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Accrual caps.</strong> Many policies cap your balance, for example
    at 1.5 times the annual amount (120 hours on an 80-hour plan). Once you
    reach the cap you stop accruing until you use some PTO, so time you
    could have earned is lost. Taking leave regularly keeps the accrual
    running.
  </p>

  <h2>What the law requires</h2>
  <h3>United States</h3>
  <p>
    Federal law doesn't require paid vacation, sick leave or holidays: the
    Fair Labor Standards Act "does not require payment for time not worked".
    PTO is a matter of employer policy or contract, plus state and local
    law. Some states and cities require paid sick leave, and rules differ on
    what happens to unused PTO when you leave. In California, vested vacation
    can't be forfeited: Labor Code §227.3 says unused vested vacation must be
    paid out at your final rate when employment ends. That rules out
    "use it or lose it" policies, though employers can stop further accrual
    with a reasonable cap.
  </p>
  <h3>United Kingdom</h3>
  <p>
    Almost all workers are entitled to 5.6 weeks' paid holiday a year, which
    is 28 days for someone working five days a week (the statutory maximum is
    28 days). Part-timers get 5.6 times their working days per week: 3 days
    a week gives 16.8 days. In the first year of employment, holiday can
    accrue at one-twelfth of the annual entitlement each month. For
    irregular-hours and part-year workers, in leave years starting on or
    after 1 April 2024, holiday accrues at <strong>12.07%</strong> of hours
    worked in each pay period: 30 hours worked earns 3.62 hours of holiday.
  </p>
  <h3>Canada (Ontario)</h3>
  <p>
    Ontario's Employment Standards Act gives at least two weeks of vacation
    time per year, rising to three weeks after five years. Vacation
    <em>pay</em> is a percentage of gross wages: at least 4%, or 6% after
    five years. On $50,000 of gross wages, 4% vacation pay is $2,000.
  </p>
  <h3>Australia</h3>
  <p>
    The National Employment Standards give full-time and part-time employees
    four weeks of paid annual leave a year, accruing progressively based on
    ordinary hours worked. A full-time employee on 38 ordinary hours a week
    earns 152 hours a year, or 1 hour for every 13 ordinary hours (0.0769
    per hour). Some shift workers get five weeks. Casual employees don't get
    paid annual leave; they receive a casual loading instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions to ask about your PTO policy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Does accrual count hours <em>worked</em> or hours <em>paid</em>? (Paid includes holidays and PTO itself.)</li>
    <li>Do overtime hours earn PTO?</li>
    <li>Is there a cap on the balance, and what happens at the cap?</li>
    <li>Can you use PTO before it's accrued, or only after?</li>
    <li>Is unused PTO paid out when you leave? (In some states it must be.)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Calculate it</h2>
  <p>
    Use the working-hours calculator on the
    <a href="/calculators/hours-worked-calculator/#per-week-month-year">Hours
    Worked page</a> to see your annual hours before and after PTO and
    holidays. The
    <a href="/calculators/salary-to-hourly-calculator/">Salary to Hourly
    Calculator</a> shows how PTO changes your real hourly rate. For background
    on annual hours, see
    <a href="/guides/how-many-work-hours-in-a-year/">how many work hours are in
    a year?</a>
  </p>

